Below is a step‐by‐step reasoning and the resulting goal model decomposition based on the mission description.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Identify the Overall Mission  
──────────────────────────────
• The mission is to “collect the required resources from storage and deliver them to the requesting agent in a specified location.”  
• This is an overall objective that must be achieved, so we define a top‐level goal (G1) as an “Achieve” goal.  
• Its target condition is: “All required resources are collected from storage and delivered to the destination.”

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Decompose the Mission into Major Phases  
──────────────────────────────
• There are two major phases:  
 – Collection phase  
 – Delivery phase  
• Both phases must complete successfully (an AND relationship).  
• In each phase, there is a “normal” behavior and an alternative (“fallback”) behavior triggered by low battery conditions.  

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Define Sub-Goals for Collection (G2)  
──────────────────────────────
• G2 “Resource Collection” is a Perform goal (since it involves a set of operational actions).  
• It has two branches:  
 – G2.1 “Normal Collection”: the standard process (navigate to storage, send request, wait, collect).  
 – G2.2 “Battery Management in Collection”: executed if the battery drops below 10% (navigate to recharging station, recharge, reassign mission).  
• The relationship between G2.1 and G2.2 is a Fallback because the fallback is used only when the normal process cannot proceed due to low battery.

──────────────────────────────
Step 4. Define Sub-Goals for Delivery (G3)  
──────────────────────────────
• G3 “Resource Delivery” is also a Perform goal.  
• It has two branches:  
 – G3.1 “Normal Delivery”: standard delivery (navigate to destination, deliver resource).  
 – G3.2 “Battery Management in Delivery”: executed if the battery drops below 30% (navigate to checkpoint, return resource, and if failure, trigger alert).  
• Again, the relationship is Fallback: the alternative branch is executed if the normal branch is not viable.

──────────────────────────────
Step 5. Define the Overall Top-Level Goal (G1)  
──────────────────────────────
• G1 “Mission Completion” is an Achieve goal with the target condition that both collection and delivery are completed.  
• Its children are G2 and G3, and both must be fulfilled (an AND relationship).  
• Although if multiple resources/items exist the mission can be run in parallel by multiple robots, for a single mission instance the collection and delivery phases are sequential.

──────────────────────────────
Step 6. Decompose Sub-Goals into Tasks  
──────────────────────────────
• For each sub-goal (G2.1, G2.2, G3.1, G3.2) we identify concrete tasks.  
• Each task is given an ID (starting with “AT”) and is associated with a specific location and a number of robots (usually “1” because one robot performs the action).

